{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Jacob.</b> And these of the generations of Jacob — these are their settlements and their unfoldings until they came to established settlement. The first cause: 'Joseph, son of seventeen,' etc. — through this the unfolding happened and they descended to Egypt. This is the plain sense of the verse, that a matter be followed through to its conclusion. And the midrash expounds: Scripture hung the generations of Jacob on Joseph for several reasons. One: all of Jacob's labor for Laban was only for Rachel, and the radiance of Joseph's countenance resembled his, and whatever happened to Jacob happened to Joseph — this one was hated and that one was hated, this one's brother sought to kill him and that one's brothers sought to kill him; and so many more in Bereshit Rabbah. And it is further expounded in it <b>And he dwelt:</b> Jacob sought to dwell in tranquility — the wrath of Joseph sprung upon him. The righteous seek to dwell in tranquility. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Is it not enough for the righteous what is prepared for them in the World to Come, that they also seek to dwell in tranquility in this world?" "<b>And he was a lad.</b> That he would do youthful deeds — fixing his hair, rolling his eyes, so that he would appear handsome." "<b>With the sons of Bilhah.</b> That is to say, frequenting the sons of Bilhah, because his brothers would degrade them and he would draw them near." "<b>And their evil report.</b> Every evil he would see in his brothers the sons of Leah he would report to his father — that they would eat a limb from a living animal, and that they would degrade the sons of the handmaids by calling them slaves, and that they were suspected of forbidden relations. And through all three they were punished: 'They slaughtered a kid of goats' — at his sale they did not eat it alive; and for the report he told about them that they call their brothers slaves — 'Joseph was sold as a slave'; and for the forbidden relations he reported about them — 'his master's wife lifted her eyes,' etc." "<b>Their report.</b> Every term דבה is פרלדי\"ץ בלע\"ז — whatever evil he was able to speak about them, he would report." "<b>Tales.</b> From the language of ״דּוֹבֵב שִׂפְתֵי יְשֵׁנִים״ (Song 7)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {4 "the la'az is Old French parledit, 'slander/evil speech'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:29.338852066Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}